WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
"Your software works, but can everyone see why?"

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
The answer is spread across code, tests, tickets, and chat.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Source Twin puts the logic in plain English inside the same project.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Run the source twin init command.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
It creates the foundation.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
You and your coding agent choose what to describe.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
A logic file explains what the software does

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
and links that explanation to useful code and tests.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
A term file gives an important product idea, one shared meaning.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Here is the messaging app today.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Maya sends, the launch has been cancelled.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
The message appears for everyone, notifications and a webhook start.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Then she realizes it should have said rescheduled.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
So you ask the agent to give people 10 seconds to undo a message.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Before touching code, the agent reads source twin.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
It finds that delivery is connected to notifications,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
webhooks, attachments, and scheduled messages.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
You agree that recipients should not see a pending message.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Notifications and webhooks should wait.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Undo should remove pending attachments.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Scheduled messages should get the same window when they actually send.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Seeing the whole change in English reveals a problem.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Urgent admin safety alerts must not wait.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The agent adds that exception.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.000
You approve the behavior.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Now the agent follows the source twin links to the exact sending code and tests.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
The current function delivers the message and starts every side effect immediately.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The implementation changes ordinary messages to a server side pending state.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
A durable job delivers them after 10 seconds.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Undo works only while a message is pending, cancels that job, and cleans up attachments.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Safety alerts still send immediately.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Next, the agent adds readable tests.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.000
One proves that an undone message never reaches recipients, notifications, or webhooks.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Another proves that urgent safety alerts do not wait.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Now Maya sees a countdown and an undo button.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
She catches the mistake.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Nothing leaves the system, and she can send the corrected message instead.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Finally, source twin check validates the files and links.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Coverage shows what is connected.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Git shows the English, code, and tests together for review.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
So, that was source twin.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Understand your software in plain English.
